v3.8.1: the token estimate stops being wrong in both directions

Check a prompt estimated tokens as len(text)/4. Measured against o200k_base, that overcounts
English prose by 7% and Python by 25%, and UNDERCOUNTS JSON by 38% and JSONL by 36%. The sign
flips with content type, so no single divisor is right, and the worst case is the one people
paste most: structured data.

estimate_tokens() now picks the divisor from the content: 2.5 for JSON-ish (punctuation density
above 6%, or the text opens and closes with braces or brackets), 4.8 for code-ish (indentation
runs, semicolons, or the usual keywords), 4.3 otherwise. On the same three corpora the error
goes from +7.0 / +24.8 / -37.7 percent to -0.4 / +4.0 / -0.4.

Still an estimate and still labelled as one. A real tokenizer was considered and rejected:
tiktoken is a compiled Rust extension needing pip, the pure-JS tokenizers add 2.3MB to a 137KB
single-file page, and both vendors' token-counting endpoints require an API key and a network
call this tool promises never to make. OpenAI's tokenizer would also be the wrong ruler for
Claude text, which it undercounts by 22-44%.

